# Untitled

High-Entropy Metal-Organic Frameworks (HEMOFs): A New Frontier in Materials Design for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Utilization
High-entropy materials (HEMs) emerged as promising candidates for a diverse array of chemical transformations, including $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ utilization. Howeve traditional HEMs catalysts are nonporous, limiting their activity to surface sites. Designing HEMs with intrinsic porosity can open the door toward enhanced reactivity while maintaining the many benefits of high configurational entropy. Here, a synergistic experimental, analytical, and theoretical approach to design the first high-entropy metal-organic frameworks (HEMOFs) derived from polynuclear metal clusters is implemented, a novel class of porous HEMs that is highly active for $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ fixation under mild conditions and short reaction times, outperforming existing heterogeneous catalysts. HEMOFs with up to 15 distinct metals are synthesized (the highest number of metals ever incorporated into a single MOF) and, for the first time, homogenous metal mixing within individual clusters is directly observed via high-resolution scanning transmission electron microscopy. Importantly, density functional theory studies provide unprecedented insight into the electronic structures of HEMOFs, demonstrating that the density of states in heterometallic clusters is highly sensitive to metal composition. This work dramatically advances HEMOF materials design, paving the way for further exploration of HEMs and offers new avenues for the development of multifunctional materials with tailored properties for a wide range of applications.
## 1. Introduction
As the technological landscape continues to evolve, innovative advanced materials must be rationally developed to address new challenges on-demand. In particular, closing the carbon cycle via carbon capture and utilization (CCU) stands at the forefront of scientific research as a technical grand challenge.[1] Among the many possible approaches, atom-efficient processes that directly convert atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into value-added chemical feedstocks (e.g., cyclic carbonates) are likely to be an integral part of the
CCU toolkit.[2,3] Cyclic carbonates serve as green solvents and important intermediates in the production of polycarbonates and polyurethanes, highlighting their dual environmental and economic importance.[4] However, the development of highly efficient, selective, and stable catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion remains a significant challenge that needs to be addressed.
In recent years, high-entropy materials (HEMs), including metal alloys and metal oxides, have demonstrated emergent properties and garnered increased attention.[5–8] HEMs are crystalline materials containing approximately equimolar quantities of 5 or more metals, which are distributed randomly across identical crystallographic positions.[5–8] These materials benefit from high configurational entropy $(S_{c o n f})$ imparted by their multi-metallic compositions, giving rise to superior materials with high mechanical strength, remarkable thermal stability, and tunable electronic and magnetic properties.[5–8] HEMs harness the properties of diverse metal centers to provide enhanced chemical properties, including oxidation resistance and catalytic activity, through synergistic effects that are not achievable in traditional materials.[5,9–11] As such, this is an emerging research area that will continue to produce new breakthroughs in performance as the field matures. HEM catalysts have shown significant promise for a diverse array of chemical transformations, including the hydrogen evolution reaction, the oxygen evolution reaction, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, nitrogen fixation, dry reforming of methane, and various organic reactions.[5,12–14] However, one drawback of traditional HEM catalysts is that they are nonporous, which means that the majority of potential reactive sites are buried in the bulk material structures and catalysis is primarily limited to surface sites. Realizing HEMs with intrinsic porosity could open the door to improved catalyst atom efficiency and enhanced reactivity while maintaining the many benefits of high configurational entropy. Designing porosity into HEMs could additionally enhance their interactions with gas-phase substrates including $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , which is a primary driver of man-made climate change.
In the realm of porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) display unsurpassed porosity and tunability. MOFs are hybrid organic-inorganic materials constructed from metal ions or clusters linked through organic molecules with two or more metal binding groups to form polymeric crystalline structures.[15] These materials have been studied for a broad range of applications and have shown immense promise as catalysts for numerous reactions, including for the conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into valueadded products.[16] A number of MOFs have been shown to be good catalysts for the reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epoxides to produce cyclic carbonates, but these typically require high pressures, elevated temperatures, and/or long reaction times in order to be effective.[3,17–19]
Combination of two or more metals into a single MOF structure has been shown to lead to synergistic effects; for example, heterometallic MOFs have demonstrated superior catalytic activity and stability compared to monometallic analogues.[20–23] Many bimetallic and trimetallic systems have been studied,[22,24–33] and MOFs containing as many as 10 unique metals have been reported,[34] but the ratios between different metals in these materials are often highly variable, limiting the impact of the targeted functionality that arises from cooperative metal interactions. Furthermore, high-entropy metal-organic frameworks (HEMOFs), which contain 5 or more distinct metals in nearequimolar ratios, remain rare in literature, and the concept of maximizing configurational entropy in MOFs has only rarely been invoked.[34–47] Most current examples of HEMOFs rely on single metal ions or rod-like metal ion chains as the inorganic building units,[35–39,41] while many of the most stable and tunable MOFs are constructed from discrete, polynuclear metal clusters.[48,49] Historically, the use of polynuclear clusters as building blocks was crucial in the development of MOF reticular chemistry, wherein choice of the appropriate organic linker and synthetic conditions allows precise control over the distances between metal clusters and their 3D arrangements.[50–53] The clusters impart stability that is otherwise difficult to achieve, allowing the development of MOFs with exceptional porosity and, thus, maximized surface area for enhanced catalysis.[54,55] In this context, expanding the materials design space to allow the incorporation of significant amounts of 5 or more metals without segregation within stable, polynuclear cluster-based MOFs would represent an important advancement toward realizing the full potential of heterometallic MOFs.
Herein, we implement a powerful materials design strategy to synthesize the first highly stable HEMOFs derived from polynuclear metal clusters, incorporating significant levels of all elements without segregation. To establish proof-of-concept, a materials family derived from rare-earth element (REE) ions and 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (TCPB) was targeted, directing the formation of a framework (M-TCPB, $\mathbf{M}=$ metal) with high internal surface area and nonanuclear metal clusters with accessible Lewis acid sites.[29,56] This materials platform offers exquisite synthetic control via simple solvothermal synthesis, which was leveraged to develop a series of HEMOFs containing 5‒15 unique metals in significant proportions, circumventing the need for energy-intensive or specialized synthetic techniques (e.g., thermal shock synthesis).[57] Exceeding the previous record for number of metals included in a single MOF (10),[34] the 15-metal HEMOF reported here establishes a new benchmark for compositional complexity and configurational entropy in MOFs.
Given the near-infinite design space offered by HEMOFs, development of these materials must necessarily be accompanied by the evolution of complementary characterization and computational tools that enable the structure-property relationships in HEMOFs to be understood and, ultimately, controlled.[20,58] In this study, a synergistic experimental-computational approach was implemented, using a suite of complementary characterization techniques and density functional theory (DFT) calculations to carefully examine the metal distributions in HEMOFs and to demonstrate control over the structure-function interplay. For the first time, homogenous metal mixing within individual HEMOF clusters was directly observed by highresolution scanning transmission electron microscopy coupled with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (STEM-EDX). Complementary density of states (DOS) calculations demonstrated unique energy shifts within the electronic structures of the materials as the number of metals was systematically increased.
The materials reported here are permanently porous and display remarkable thermal and chemical stability. Their complex metallic compositions create a rich landscape of catalytic sites to effectively promote the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and 4- chlorostyrene oxide under mild conditions and short reaction times, outperforming existing heterogeneous catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ epoxidation. Importantly, the materials design strategy outlined here is flexible to allow exploration of a larger number of HEMOF families with related cluster compositions, but with distinct linker sizes and topologies. In combination with DFT studies, this approach supports effective predictive capabilities, enabling the direct assessment of the impact of cluster proximity, electronic structure, and overall porosity on the catalytic process. Ultimately, this represents an important advancement toward the ability to intelligently target specific metallic compositions in the design of high-performance HEMs.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. HEMOF Synthesis and Characterization
Synthesis of heterometallic MOFs requires judicious choice of metals to avoid phase segregation, and REE ions display superior compatibility with one another in comparison to other classes of metals (e.g., transition metals).[24,59–62] There are 17 REEs, including the lanthanides (La−Lu), Y, and Sc. Of these 17 elements, 15 were deemed viable for this study (Table 1). Promethium was eliminated due to its radioactivity, while scandium was excluded due to its smaller ionic radius relative to the other REEs. Because M-TCPB is only known to form with $^{3+}$ metals, REEs in the $^{3+}$ oxidation state were used exclusively in this work.
The inorganic building units of M-TCPB are nonanuclear clusters of $^{3+}$ REE ions that are bridged through two $\upmu_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{O}^{2-}$ ligands and twelve ${\upmu}_{3}$ -bridging $\mathrm{OH^{-}/F^{-}}$ ligands (Figure 1A).[29,56] Fluoride and hydroxide occupy interchangeable crystallographic positions and may exist in variable ratios, but it is noteworthy that these clusters do not form in the absence of a fluoride source.[64,65] In this case, $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ is derived from the decomposition of a modulator, 2-fluorobenzoic acid (2-FBA).[64] All nine REE ions display one terminal $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligand, providing numerous potential open metal sites. The clusters are capped by twelve $\upmu_{2}$ -bridging carboxylates from tetratopic TCPB linkers, forming a (4,12)- connected net with triangular channels running parallel to the crystallographic c-axis (Figure 1A). M-TCPB can be formed with numerous REE ions, making it an ideal platform for this study.[29]
In order to assess the viability of forming HE M-TCPB, a 5- metal composition with Eu, Gd, Er, Yb, and Y was initially targeted. M-TCPB compositions containing Y (monometallic)[56] and Eu, Gd, and Yb (trimetallic)[29] have been reported previously, guiding the choice of these four metals, and Er was chosen as a fifth metal that falls in the size range between ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+}$ $(\mathrm{r_{ion}}=1.066$ and $0.985\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , respectively). Direct synthesis was attempted from solutions containing equimolar ratios of all five metals (from either chloride or nitrate salts). Reaction of TCPB with the five metal precursors, 2-fluorobenzoic acid, and $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ in DMF at $115^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three days yielded colorless crystals as hexagonal plates. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) confirmed the formation of the desired M-TCPB material, denoted as 5MTCPB, with high crystallinity and phase purity (Figure 1B, burgundy trace). $\Nu_{2}$ physisorption analysis of an activated sample at 77 K confirmed that 5M-TCPB is permanently porous, with a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $1520\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 1C, burgundy). The five corresponding monometallic derivatives (Eu-, Gd-, Er-, Yb-, and Y-TCPB) were also synthesized by the same procedure, giving comparable characterization data in each case (Figure 1B,C).
Scanning electron microscopy coupled with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (SEM/EDX) analysis of 5M-TCPB revealed homogeneous incorporation of all five metals into each MOF crystal (Figure 1D). A 5M-TCPB sample was digested and analyzed by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) in order to determine the precise molar ratios of the metals, and it was found that each individual metal was incorporated in a significant proportion (Table 1). Among the lanthanides, the heavier (smaller) ions were incorporated somewhat preferentially, while the percentage of Y in the MOF was found to be higher than the input ratio. The larger than expected amount of Y was attributed to its higher reactivity as the only transition metal in the series.
HEMs should contain near-equimolar ratios of 5 or more metals. The ideal configurational entropy $(S_{c o n f})$ of an HEM can be approximated as:
$$
{S_{c o n f}\approx-R\sum x_{i}\mathrm{ln}\left(x_{i}\right)}
$$
where $x_{i}$ is the mole fraction of each metal relative to the total metal content and $\mathtt{R}$ is the ideal gas constant, and one definition of an HEM is a material in which $S_{c o n f}\geq1.5\mathrm{R}$ .[5,6] MOFs contain large numbers of nonmetal atoms and there are therefore inherent shortcomings in the application of Equation 1 to HEMOFs; however, considering the $S_{c o n f}$ based on metal composition alone was deemed sufficient for comparison between different HEMOFs in the context of this work. Despite some variation from the ideal equimolar metal ratios, the $S_{c o n f}$ of 5M-TCPB was calculated as $1.54\mathrm{R}$ using Equation 1 and the experimentallyderived ratios (from ICP-MS analysis), qualifying the material as a successful example of a HEMOF.
To test the limits of the HEMOF design, direct synthesis of M-TCPB containing all fifteen of the metals identified in Table 1 was attempted. The reaction conditions were identical to the conditions used to generate 5M-TCPB with an additional ten metal salts included in the reaction solution. Crystals with the same morphology as 5M-TCPB were obtained, and PXRD confirmed the formation of a phase-pure M-TCPB material (Figure 1B, light blue trace), denoted as 15M-TCPB. The average crystal size was observed to decrease from 5M-TCPB to 15M-TCPB (Figure 1D,E), likely due to differences in reactivity between the different mixtures of metals. 15M-TCPB displays high porosity comparable to 5M-TCPB and the monometallic M-TCPB MOFs $(\mathrm{S}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{BET}}=1230{-}1580\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ with a $\Nu_{2}$ BET surface area of $1360\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 1C and Table 2). The differences in BET surface areas among different M-TCPB compositions can be largely attributed to differences in the average atomic masses of the metal ions incorporated. Amazingly, SEM-EDX confirmed that each of the 15 metals was incorporated in a significant quantity, with a homogeneous distribution of the metals across the single crystal length scale (Figure 1E). It should be noted, however, that each cluster can contain a maximum of 9 different metals, necessitating some compositional variation between different clusters. ICP-MS analysis of a digested sample of 15M-TCPB revealed that the lanthanides each accounted for ca. $3.7\%-10.0\%$ of the total metal content (equimolar $=6.7\%$ each), while Y accounted for $14.3\%$ (Table 1). The results agree well with the percentages determined for 5M-TCPB, with smaller lanthanide ions generally favored over larger ones and Y favored over the lanthanides. To the best of our knowledge, 15 is the largest number of individual metals ever incorporated into a single MOF structure. The $S_{c o n f}$ of 15M-TCPB was calculated as 2.63R, far exceeding the value for 5M-TCPB. This result pushes the boundaries of the HEMOFs concept, demonstrating that the configurational entropy of M-TCPB can be maximized over a vast compositional space. Notably, HE M-TCPB materials with 6‒14 unique REE metals were also successfully obtained by direct synthesis according to PXRD data (Figure S1, Supporting Information).
Further refinement of PXRD data for monometallic M-TCPB $(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Y},$ Eu, Gd, Er, Yb) revealed a trend wherein the smaller REE ions give lower calculated unit cell volumes (Figure S2 and Table S1, Supporting Information), ranging from 10187– 10669 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ (Yb−Eu). This was expected due to the corresponding shortening of REE−carboxylate coordination bonds as metal ionic radius is decreased. Significantly, the calculated unit cell volumes of 5M-TCPB and 15M-TCPB (10424 and 10521 Å3, respectively) were found to have intermediate values (Table S1, Supporting Information), showing an averaging effect. This is direct evidence that the various metals in 5M- and 15M-TCPB exist in a homogeneous heterometallic phase without segregation.
MOFs are typically less robust than other common heterogeneous materials (e.g., metal oxides, zeolites, etc.), which is one factor that has limited their widespread industrial implementation.[66] Although thousands of MOF structures have been reported, application development in the MOF field has largely focused on a handful of platforms that are known to be highly stable (e.g., UiO-66, MIL-101, ZIF-8, etc.). Because new materials must be stable to be viable for practical applications, the thermal and aqueous stability of M-TCPB was thoroughly evaluated. Two HE M-TCPB compositions (5M- and 15M-TCPB) were evaluated along with 5 monometallic analogues (Y-, Eu-, Gd-, Er-, and Yb-TCPB). MOF samples were soaked in liquid water for a minimum of two months in air and evaluated by PXRD at various time points (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Impressively, M-TCPB was found to retain a high degree of crystallinity in water regardless of composition or exposure time. Many other REEbased MOFs suffer from poor water stability, demonstrating the beneficial impact of the fluorinated REE clusters in M-TCPB. Furthermore, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) demonstrated that the materials have excellent thermal stability, with decomposition temperatures beyond $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The high thermal stability of M-TCPB can also be attributed to the robust fluorinated REE building units. The various M-TCPB compositions gave similar results, displaying three distinct weight loss events in most cases. In order to determine the origins of these weight losses, 5M-TCPB was chosen as an exemplar and studied by TGA coupled with mass spectrometry (TGA-MS) under helium (Figure S5, Supporting Information). This revealed that the first mass loss between room temperature and ${\approx}150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ corresponded to loss of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , which is presumably adsorbed within the MOF pores. The second feature, beginning at ${\approx}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , correlated with the detection of increased quantities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO in the mass spectrum. This relatively minor weight loss is most likely the result of decomposition of small organic molecules trapped in the pore structure. Importantly, these first two weight loss features do not indicate decomposition of the MOF structure, but rather desorption/decomposition of guest molecules. MOF decomposition occurred at ${\approx}550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ via decarboxylation of the organic linkers, as evidenced by the detection of large quantities of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO by MS. CO is presumably detected as a fragmentation product of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ formed during MS ionization.
In order to fully take advantage of heterometallic compositions, it is crucial to control the location of individual metal ions with respect to one another, as the distance between ions determines the extent to which they may function cooperatively and/or synergistically.[20,67] As such, the formation of monometallic domains, wherein there is some segregation of metals over smaller (nm) length scales, is typically detrimental.[31] Additionally, any deviation from a random, homogeneous metal distribution decreases $S_{c o n f}$ with respect to the ideal value.[6] SEM-EDX across a broad range of 5M- and 15M-TCPB crystals (Figures S6 and S7, Supporting Information, respectively) demonstrated compositional homogeneity between different crystals and across individual crystals, but this technique lacks sufficient resolution to evaluate short-range metal distributions. Given this challenge, rigorous spectroscopic characterization of M-TCPB materials was undertaken to determine the degrees of metal mixing.
Due to the challenges associated with interpreting spectral data for large numbers of metals, a series of 2- and 3-metal MTCPB MOFs was synthesized containing every possible combination of Eu, Yb, and Y. These three metals were chosen to include two lanthanides of different sizes and Y, which is a transition metal and incorporates preferentially over other metals. These elements also have distinct, non-overlapping electron binding energies, enabling characterization of the materials by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, vide infra). Input ratios of individual metals were equimolar in each case to maximize the degrees of potential metal mixing. Using the standard M-TCPB synthetic protocol, 4 materials were obtained: EuYb-, EuY-, YbY-, and EuYbY-TCPB. Successful synthesis of these bi- and trimetallic M-TCPB materials was confirmed by PXRD (Figure 1B), which indicated the formation of phase pure, isostructural materials in every case. SEM-EDX confirmed successful incorporation of the targeted metals in each material (Figures S8–S11, Supporting Information), and ICP-MS analysis of digested samples revealed the precise molar ratios of the different metals (Table S2, Supporting Information). The bi- and trimetallic MOFs followed the same compositional trend as the HEMOFs, with Y favored over the two lanthanides and Yb (the smaller lanthanide) favored over Eu. Nevertheless, the metallic compositions of the materials ensured ample opportunities for metal mixing.
## 2.2. Spectroscopic Studies
It has been demonstrated that spectroscopic techniques, including XPS and infrared (IR) spectroscopy, can be used to distinguish between well-mixed and segregated metal distributions.[20,22,27] Because XPS binding energies are sensitive to coordination environment, mixed-metal clusters are expected to display shifted XPS peaks relative to the corresponding monometallic clusters (Figure 2 inset).[22] This should be true for both the metals and any bridging ions (e.g., $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ). In heterometallic MOFs, this allows the differentiation of materials with mixed-metal clusters from MOFs in which different metals are segregated into separate clusters.[22] In order to make this determination, monometallic derivatives containing each of the metals of interest must be synthesized and analyzed in addition to the heterometallic MOF. Comparison to the single-metal standards can be used to determine whether the metal peaks are shifted in the mixed-metal material. It is important that the metals in question display non-overlapping, well resolved XPS peaks for this analysis.
XPS was used to evaluate the degrees of metal mixing in heterometallic M-TCPB materials. The mono-, bi-, and trimetallic MTCPB MOFs containing Eu, Yb, and Y (Eu-, Yb-, Y-, EuYb-, EuY-, YbY-, and EuYbY-TCPB) were analyzed to observe the behavior of these 3 well resolved elements, and 5M-TCPB was also analyzed to observe any differences in behavior based on the HE configuration. Eu 3d, Yb 4d, Y 3d, and survey XPS spectra were collected for the full suite of materials (Figure 2; Figures S12–S21, Supporting Information). Because fluoride ions bridge adjacent metals in the MOF clusters, F 1s spectra were also collected for each of these materials plus Gd- and Er-TCPB (Figure 2). Binding energies were tracked across the series to quantify peak shifts, with the envelopes used to identify peak maxima and peak positions.
The largest deviations were observed for Eu, which is the largest and most polarizable cation in the series, while the changes in peak positions for $\mathrm{Yb}$ and Y were less pronounced. Shifting of one or more metal peaks by at least $\pm0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ was observed for each bimetallic composition, indicating the presence of mixed-metal clusters in all 3 bimetallic materials.[22] Each of the 3 metal peaks shifted by ${\approx}0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ in EuYbY-TCPB, also indicating intra-cluster metal mixing. In 5M-TCPB, the Y 3d peak was shifted $-0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to Y-TCPB, while the Yb 4d binding energy was nearly identical to that of monometallic Yb-TCPB.
The most significant difference in 5M-TCPB was seen in the Eu 3d binding energies. The primary peak shifted $-0.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to Eu-TCPB while also developing a pronounced shoulder. Although the origin of the shoulder is unclear, the data suggest that Eu exists primarily in heterometallic clusters. Taken together, the Eu and Y data point to high degrees of metal mixing in 5MTCPB, while the minimal deviation of the Yb 4d peak position was consistent with other mixed-metal compositions (i.e., YbYand EuYbY-TCPB).
As expected based on relative electronegativity values,[68] Y-TCPB displayed the highest F 1s binding energy in the monometallic series. Surprisingly, EuY- and YbY-TCPB gave F binding energies essentially identical to Y-TCPB, indicating a predominance of Y-F-Y motifs beyond what would be expected for a random fluoride distribution. In the case of EuY-TCPB, this may be partially attributed to the higher Y:Eu ratio (ca. 4:1). F 1s binding energies consistently decreased when moving to materials containing 3 and 5 metals, shifting to energies that were more representative of average values as the mole fraction of Y decreased. This averaging indicates that fluoride and metal distributions become more random as the number of unique metals is increased.
Interestingly, peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ were observed in each Eu-containing sample, despite the use of an ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ precursor in the MOF syntheses. The $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ peak was much more pronounced in 5M-TCPB compared to the other materials, again pointing to the existence of complex Eu environments. The presence of $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ was posited to be a surface phenomenon and was investigated further by hard $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (HAXPES).[69]
One main shortcoming of XPS analysis of MOFs is the small penetration depth, which is typically less than $10\mathrm{nm}$ . This means XPS spectra only reflect the nature of species near the crystal surfaces. In order to ascertain whether the XPS spectra of these materials reflect the compositions of the crystal interiors, several M-TCPB MOFs (Eu-, Yb-, Y-, and EuYbY-TCPB) were studied by HAXPES (Figures S22–S24, Supporting Information), which allows the collection of depth-dependent data.[70] Importantly, the spectra collected deeper in the materials matched well with the surface spectra for Y and $\mathrm{Yb}$ , indicating minimal depth dependence for these metals. However, the Eu 3d HAXPES spectra were a notable exception. While $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ was clearly observed in Eu- and EuYbY-TCPB at low penetration depths, little to no $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ was found deeper into the crystals. Thus, the formation of $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ was ascribed to redox chemistry at the crystal surfaces, while the bulk crystals contain only $\mathtt{E u}^{3+}$ .[69] The ability of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to undergo reduction to $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ in REE cluster based MOFs has been posited previously but not unambiguously demonstrated until now.[71]
When analyzed alongside complementary data, IR spectroscopy can yield additional evidence of metal mixing. The $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ vibrational energies of bridging hydroxide ions are sensitive to the properties of the coordinated metal ions, such that changing the metals gives a small but measurable shift in the corresponding IR peaks. In this case, M1-OH-M1 and M2-OH-M2 (M1 and M2 are generic metal ions, $\mathbf{M}1\neq\mathbf{M}2$ ) would show $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ IR peaks at different energies, and the energies of the M1-OH$\mathbf{M}2\delta\mathrm{OH}$ vibrations are expected to occur at intermediate values (Figure S25A, Supporting Information).[27] It should be noted that the positions and intensities of $_\mathrm{O-H}$ IR peaks may be sensitive to environmental factors, including relative humidity, but inspection of IR spectra has been used to verify random metal mixing in previous reports.[27] All M-TCPB materials were studied by IR (Figure S25B and C, Supporting Information), with specific attention paid to the $\delta\mathrm{OH}$ energies $(\approx845~\mathrm{cm^{-1}};$ ), as the $\nu\mathrm{O-H}$ bands $(\approx3750~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ were not well resolved enough to provide meaningful information. All IR spectra were collected during the same ${\approx}2\mathrm{~h~}$ time period to minimize the impact of environmental factors. By comparison to the monometallic MOFs, the majority of the mixed-metal M-TCPB materials display intermediate $\delta\mathrm{OH}$ peaks, indicating intra-cluster metal mixing. The only exception is EuY-TCPB, which gave a $\delta\mathrm{OH}$ peak at an energy nearly identical to that of Eu-TCPB. This points to a predominance of Eu-OH-Eu motifs greater than that expected for a completely random distribution. XPS analysis of EuY-TCPB (above) additionally indicated an excess of Y-F-Y motifs. Although $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ and $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ should function interchangeably in the MOF clusters, these data indicate a degree of anion segregation in EuY-TCPB, wherein $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ preferentially coordinates to Y and $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ preferentially coordinates to Eu. This necessitates some segregation of the metals as well, although the exact domain structure is unclear. Significantly, no evidence of metal or anion segregation was seen in the IR data for 5M- and 15MTCPB.
## 2.3. High Resolution STEM-EDX
High resolution TEM/STEM have been used to image MOF structures on an atomic level.[72,73] This has allowed direct visual observation of structural features, including defects and surface terminations. Additionally, STEM has been coupled with EDX to inform elemental composition across small length scales in MOFs.[74–76] STEM-EDX is a powerful tool that has given insight into metal mixing in other HE materials (e.g., oxides and alloys) with high resolution.[5,7,57] However, MOFs are more susceptible to beam damage than traditional HE materials, and STEM-EDX of mixed-metal MOFs has not been reported with sufficient resolution to visualize intra-cluster metal mixing. To address this research gap, STEM-EDX analysis was performed on 5M-TCPB targeting cluster level resolution. Fortunately, a sufficiently thin MOF layer with the proper orientation was identified, and analysis yielded conclusive evidence of a well-mixed metal distribution in the material (Figure 3A). EDX demonstrated that each individual cluster that was imaged contains all 5 of the REE ions in the material. Well-defined regions of high concentration were observed in overlapping regions for each metal, corresponding to the individual nonanuclear clusters. The data for Eu was somewhat less well defined, which may be attributed to the low Eu content $(11.3\mathrm{mol\%})$ relative to the other metals, but still showed a similar clustering effect. Furthermore, the data was effectively fit using multivariate statistical analysis with two compositional components, one for the REE clusters and one for the organic portion, confirming that each cluster has approximately the same composition (Figure 3B).[77,78] Based on this result, STEM-EDX may prove to be a superior method for the determination of shortrange metal distributions in heterometallic MOFs. STEM-EDX of 15M-TCPB did not yield the complete cluster-level resolution seen for 5M-TCPB but did confirm homogeneous metal mixing over the short length scales examined (Figure 3C). Nevertheless, machine learning analysis was performed on two separate regions of 15M-TCPB and did indicate compositional homogeneity across the array of heterometallic clusters (Figure S26, Supporting Information).
## 2.4. Catalytic $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Conversion
Conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into value-added products is a promising method to combat the rising atmospheric concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , which is one of the primary causes of climate change.[79] $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be used as a raw material for the production of cyclic carbonates, which are important green solvents and intermediates in the synthesis of pharmaceuticals and polymers.[4] This conversion can be achieved by reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epoxides in the presence of a Lewis acid catalyst, typically with a halide source as a co-catalyst to facilitate epoxide ring-opening.[2,3] Many efficient catalysts for these transformations are known,[2] but MOFs have emerged as some of the most promising candidates.[3] Many MOFs display potential open metal sites on their inorganic building units, which can serve as catalytic sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ epoxidation.
MOFs constructed using strong Lewis acids, such as $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ , are some of the most efficient examples.[19,80] However, elevated temperatures and pressures and/or long reaction times are often needed to achieve significant conversion.[18,19,81,82]
Because HEMOFs have unique electronic structures and structural flexibility, they may prove to be superior heterogeneous catalysts. The high degrees of metal mixing in various M-TCPB compositions maximize the opportunities for different metals to function synergistically and/or cooperatively. REE ions can function effectively as Lewis acid catalysts,[83] and the predominance of potential open metal sites (i.e., REE ions with terminal $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligands) on the 9-metal clusters in M-TCPB should yield high catalytic activity. However, MOFs based on polynuclear REE clusters have only rarely been used as Lewis acid catalysts.[82] Given these considerations, 5M- and 15M-TCPB were evaluated as heterogeneous catalysts for the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with 4- chlorostyrene oxide under mild conditions (1 atm $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; Scheme 1). Although it is not commonly used as a model substrate for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions, 4-chlorostyrene oxide displays lower toxicity than many other epoxides (e.g., styrene oxide) and is less volatile, making it more straightforward to work with. Importantly, although many MOF catalysts become less efficient with bulky epoxides, the pores in M-TCPB are sufficiently large $(1.0{-}1.5\mathrm{nm})$ to accommodate 4-chlorostyrene oxide.
M-TCPB and tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBA-Br) were added to a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ Schlenk flask under inert atmosphere, then
4-chlorostyrene oxide was added as a neat liquid. The headspace of the flask was flushed with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ then sealed under a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ balloon, with a pressure of ${\approx}1$ atm. The reaction was heated to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ and the reaction products were quantified by $^1\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), with hexamethylbenzene used as an internal standard (Figures S27–S58, Supporting Information). Similar to many other systems, TBA-Br was used as a co-catalyst to facilitate the catalytic cycle.[3,19,82] To better evaluate the differences in reactivity between different M-TCPB compositions, we chose a reaction time $(6\mathrm{h})$ designed to achieve $<100\%$ conversion $(\approx70\%)$ . In addition to the HEMOFs, the 5 monometallic materials (Eu-, Gd-, Er-, Yb-, and Y-TCPB) and EuYbY-TCPB were individually assessed, allowing us to correlate composition with reactivity. It should be noted that all M-TCPB materials were synthesized via the same experimental procedure, including the same equivalence and concentration of the FBA modulator and ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , and gave similar $\Nu_{2}$ sorption performance. Accordingly, defect character should be similar across the M-TCPB series, meaning differences in performance can largely be attributed to compositional variation. The results of all catalytic testing are presented in Table 2.
Despite the mild conditions and relatively short reaction time, all M-TCPB compositions gave $265\%$ conversion after $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . Of the monometallic compositions, Y-TCPB gave the highest $6\mathrm{~h~}$ conversion at $73\%$ . Among the monometallic lanthanide materials, conversion roughly trended with ionic radius (Table 1), with Yb$(72\%)$ and Er-TCPB $(68\%)$ showing higher conversion than Eu$(66\%)$ and Gd-TCPB $(65\%)$ . This trend can be explained by the stronger Lewis acidity of the smaller (harder) ions. EuYbY-TCPB $(70\%)$ and 5M-TCPB $(69\%)$ both displayed intermediate performance that was in close agreement with the average activity of the metal components. Interestingly, 15M-TCPB ( $72\%$ conversion) showed enhanced activity relative to 5M-TCPB, making it one of the most active compositions. This result is surprising given the activity trend with REE ionic radius, as the average ionic radius of the metals in 15M-TCPB is on par with that of ${\mathrm{~Gd}}^{3+}$ $(\approx1.05\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , which formed the least active material in the M-TCPB series (GdTCPB). Based on this outcome, 15M-TCPB performs better than the sum of its parts would suggest, providing evidence for a synergistic effect in this material. Although 15M-TCPB did not outperform the material with the highest 6 h conversion (Y-TCPB), it is noteworthy that combination of Y with 14 other metals (which are known or expected to be less active than Y) gave a material with near-equal activity to Y-TCPB. High-entropy 5- and 15M-TCPB were also evaluated over an $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ timeframe, with the longer reaction time giving near-complete conversion and highlighting the potential utility of these materials. Importantly, all M-TCPB materials retained full crystallinity after the catalytic process by PXRD, further confirming the high stability of the M-TCPB platform (Figure S59, Supporting Information) and suggesting that the materials should be recyclable. Follow-up studies are ongoing to confirm the recyclability of M-TCPB and further evaluate the interplay between metal composition and catalytic performance.
To gain insight into the catalytic mechanism, DFT calculations were employed to determine the geometries and relative energies of the reaction intermediates and transition states. The full details of these calculations and associated figures and data are shown in the supporting information (Figures S60–S63 and Table S3, Supporting Information). The primary activation barriers were found to be ring opening of the epoxide and ring closing to form the final product, and the intermediate geometries agreed with previously hypothesized reaction mechanisms.[41,84,85] These findings indicate that strategies for further enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ epoxidation catalytic activity should revolve around lowering the activation energies for these steps, which may be achievable via tuning of metal composition.
The landscape of reaction conditions for MOF-catalyzed epoxidation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in literature is vast, with many different temperatures, pressures, reaction times, and substrates used. This is a significant challenge when attempting to compare different materials based on literature reports. In order to address this challenge, benchmark reactions were performed with several other materials from the MOF and zeolite families. M-TCPB significantly outperformed commercial zeolites, which are more traditional porous materials that are used as catalysts in industrial processes. The conversion for silica-alumina zeolite $(26\%)$ was essentially on par with the control reaction with no heterogeneous catalyst $(25\%)$ , while the conversion for Y-zeolite was only slightly higher $(32\%)$ . The M-TCPB results were further benchmarked by comparison to two well-known $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ MOFs, MOF-808[86] and UiO-66,[87] under identical reaction conditions. Although $\mathrm{zr}$ - MOFs have been widely studied as Lewis acid catalysts,[80] both UiO-66 and MOF-808 gave substantially lower conversion $(56\%-$ $57\%)$ than the entire series of M-TCPB materials. These benchmarks and literature examples[3] strongly suggest that M-TCPB is a superior catalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ epoxidation, achieving high conversion with a bulky substrate under mild conditions and short timeframes. The superior M-TCPB performance can be attributed to the abundance of open metal sites on the nonanuclear REE clusters. Beyond that, catalytic activity was found to be composition dependent, so our enhanced ability to incorporate any number of REE metals into the M-TCPB framework will facilitate further optimization going forward. While HEMOFs with equimolar metal ratios were the focus of this work, it is important to consider that having an excess of one metal (e.g., Y) may yield enhanced catalytic activity. Additionally, based on the prevalence and accessibility of Lewis acidic REE ions in M-TCPB, these materials are likely to be active for a range of Lewis acid-catalyzed reactions.
## 3. Computational Modeling
The ability to homogeneously integrate up to fifteen metals into a single MOF opens up a near-limitless design space in which important properties are directly dependent upon the tunable electronic structures of the materials. In particular, electronic DOS calculations can give invaluable insight into material properties, including catalytic activity, band structure, conductivity, and optical properties. When applied to hypothetical materials, computational screening can accelerate the discovery of new materials for important applications. This is especially valuable when working in vast design spaces with countless possibilities, such as the compositional space offered by HEMs.[88,89] Computational methods will therefore be vital to the continued development of the HEM field.
While DFT calculations can give a detailed understanding of the DOS in materials, these calculations are hindered by large numbers of atoms. For DFT to be viable for complex materials, it must be performed on representative models containing the smallest possible number of atoms. In MOFs, this is often accomplished by modeling the inorganic building unit as a discrete molecular cluster, which can serve as an excellent proxy to understand the properties of the extended materials. Our previous work has shown that monometallic RE MOFs have unique optical responses to the adsorption of specific molecular species[90] and that trimetallic RE MOFs exhibit photocontrol via charge transfer pathways.[29] With these previous results providing the groundwork, DFT calculations were employed to gain further insight into the unique electronic environments in heterometallic M-TCPB materials as a function of composition, including high-entropy 5M-TCPB (see supporting information for computational details). Although up to 15 metals can be incorporated into one M-TCPB material, the number of metals was capped at 5 for computational feasibility. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of detailed DFT calculations for a MOF (cluster) containing 5 unique metals, overcoming the pronounced computational hurdles posed by HEMOFs.
The models were built as nonanuclear clusters analogous to those in M-TCPB with twelve terminal formate ligands in place of the linker-based carboxylates. The two central ${\upmu}_{3}$ -bridging ligands were modeled as ${{0}^{2-}}$ and the other twelve $\upmu_{3}$ -bridging ligands were modeled as $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ , although there is some disordered mixing of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ in the material. The nine terminal ligands were modeled as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , giving the cluster an overall $^{-1}$ charge. Model clusters were built containing mono-, bi-, and trimetallic combinations of Eu, Yb, and Y, matching the materials used for XPS analysis, as well as 5M-TCPB (Table 3). The monometallic clusters were modeled to provide valuable baseline information about the electronic structures of M-TCPB MOFs, while heterometallic clusters were investigated to give additional insight into the effects of compositional variation. The metal ratios were maintained at or near equimolar, with the experimentally determined molar percentages (Table 1; Table S2, Supporting Information) used to determine which elements should be in slight excess as dictated by the odd number of metal ions (9). The metal sites were randomly populated for each composition to give arrangements that should be favored by direct heterometallic synthesis. It is important to note that experimental evidence unequivocally demonstrated incorporation of all 5 metals into each REE cluster in 5M-TCPB (vide supra), validating the use of a single-cluster model.
To analyze the electronic structure environments in mono- and heterometallic RE MOFs, calculated projected densities of states (PDOS) were visualized to show individual metal contributions to the total electronic structures (Figure 4). This type of analysis was previously used to understand the importance of metal ratios and positioning on the electronic structures of bimetallic actinide MOFs.[91] The contribution of Ln 4f orbitals to chemical bonds and interactions are generally minimal, with the largest change to the metal PDOS occurring in the 4d orbitals.[92] With that in mind, the PDOS in Figure 4 represent only the electron densities localized on the RE metal elements as a function of energy. The total DOS data is available in the supporting information (Figure S64, Supporting Information).
In comparing mono- versus heterometallic PDOS, a number of interesting trends become apparent. First, individual PDOS for monometallic Eu, Yb, and Y clusters show similar features, with ${\mathrm{Yb}} $ presenting a stronger peak near the valence band edge (VBE, Figure 4 green region) and a reduced conduction band edge (CBE, Figure 4 yellow region) compared to both Y and Eu. Second, the relative behaviors of Eu, Yb, and Y change upon incorporation into bimetallic clusters. In $\mathrm{Eu}_{4}\mathrm{Yb}_{5}$ , Eu and Yb give relatively equivalent contributions to the PDOS at both the VBE and CBE, and the peak amplitudes for both elements are reshaped near $-0.13$ and $-0.6\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Similarly, Eu and Y contribute essentially equally to the PDOS in the $\mathrm{Eu}_{4}\mathrm{Y}_{5}$ model, with the monometallic amplitude patterns largely conserved in this case. On the other hand, while the CBE of ${\tt Y b}_{5}{\tt Y}_{4}$ receives approximately equal contributions from Yb and Y, the VBE is dominated by Yb, which also shows moderate delocalization near the VBE. Interestingly, the trimetallic cluster $(\Upsilon_{3}\Upsilon\mathsf{b}_{3}\mathtt{E u}_{3})$ shows different changes in relative PDOS between the three metal species. Yb remains the primary contributor to the VBE while Eu shifts density to its second peak in the VB at $-0.67\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The most notable change occurs in the PDOS of Y, which shifts $-0.3\mathrm{eV}$ below the Fermi energy in the VB while nonetheless contributing significantly to the CBE. Notably, the Y states shift to energies better correlated with localization on the organic linkers, which could be important for optical absorption and charge transfer events.
The intermetallic effects are also pronounced in the 5M model cluster (Figures 4 and 5A; Figure S65, Supporting Information), which includes Y, Eu, Gd, Er, and Yb. By comparison to the EuYbY cluster, inclusion of Er and Gd results in additional relative energy shifts within the electronic structure (Figures 4 and 5). The VBE of the 5M cluster is dominated by contributions from Yb and Y, while Eu, Gd, and Er are shifted below the Fermi level by $0.25\mathrm{eV}.$ This behavior is in direct contrast to the behavior of Y in the EuYbY model, which shifts below the Fermi level and gives essentially no contribution to the VBE. Only the 3- and 5-metal systems exhibit shifting of specific metals into the VB and away from the Fermi level, which suggests that multi-metallic compositions can be used to enable fine control over electronic structures. The CBE of the 5M cluster receives contributions from all 5 elements but is largely localized on Er at $4.07\mathrm{eV},$ with Er amplitudes ${\approx}3.5\mathrm{x}{-}17\mathrm{x}$ higher than the other metals. Interestingly, the opposite is seen for the second peak in the CB $(\approx4.8\mathrm{eV})$ , where Er has the lowest contribution of all 5 metals and considerable localization is seen on Gd. The contributions of Eu, Yb, and Y become more significant at higher CB energies, with Eu generally being the least prominent of the three.
The shifts and relative localization of electron density of the metal species (e.g., Figure 4) are important when considering multiple physical and chemical processes that occur within RE MOF materials (e.g., photophysics, charge transfer and transport mechanisms, chemical adsorption, and catalytic reactivity);[93–95] however, in the total DOS for 5M-TCPB, the localization on the RE metals is relatively low compared to the p-orbital contributions from O, F, and C, which comprise the formate, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ , and $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ligands (Figure 5A). The total p-orbital DOS in Figure 5A has been normalized at the VBE for ease of visualization and otherwise dwarfs the metal contributions. Oxygen atoms from the formate and ${\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ ligands contribute significantly to the total p-orbital DOS in the VB (Figure 5), which is unsurprising given the important role of occupied O p-orbitals in forming the metal−ligand coordination bonds that comprise the cluster, and the VBE largely resides on the central ${{0}^{2-}}$ ligands. The two metals that contribute to the VBE are $\mathrm{Yb}$ and Y, which form more bonds to the ${{0}^{2-}}$ ligands (2) than the other metals. This further demonstrates the importance of the central ${{0}^{2-}}$ to the localization of the VBE, which corresponds with the location of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO). Conversely, d-orbitals on the metals with fewer bonds to ${\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ (Eu, Gd, and Er) and p-orbitals from the formate atoms are the primary components of the CBE. Eu, Gd, and Er also begin to contribute lower in the VB, with a corresponding increase in the O DOS below the VBE. The resonance in energy between O and the metal species indicates possible pathways for metal-to-ligand or ligand-to-metal charge transfer events, which would be tunable through variation of metal species and ratios. These factors will also have a considerable impact on catalytic events and processes.
Ultimately, these computational results reveal the importance of compositional control in HEMOF clusters; compared to the simpler mono- and bimetallic systems, more complex compositions (i.e., larger numbers of metals) displayed unique energy shifts in their electronic structures with respect to the simpler mono- and bimetallic systems. Adsorption and catalysis largely take place at active metal sites within the MOFs, which directly impacts the local electronic structure of the participating metals. Because different metal species comprise the VBE and CBE, chemical events taking place at specific metal centers may result in easily differentiated observations. The detailed computational analysis performed here can serve as a foundation to further resolve the impacts of metal compositions and distributions on experimental properties, which can then be applied for the rational design of the next generation of high-performance HEMOFs.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, the successful realization of a family of tailorable, highly porous HEMOF catalyst materials derived from polynuclear metal clusters was described herein. We demonstrated the first composition of a 15-metal HEMOF, achieving an unparalleled level of complexity and an important advancement in this nascent field. Rigorous application of analytical techniques combined with computational modeling unambiguously demonstrated that the metals in these materials are well mixed at the cluster level, maximizing their configurational entropy. All reported materials demonstrated outstanding catalytic activity for the epoxidation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , which is critical for the development of highly efficient next-generation CCU materials. Our computational methods also enabled a detailed understanding of the electronic structures of these materials, paving the way for more advanced applications of HEMOFs. This work is a significant advancement in HEMOFs design that opens up a new frontier in catalysis for sustainable chemical synthesis. Our methods can accelerate the exploration of HEMs for a wide range of applications that require enhanced multifunctionality and performance.
## 5. Experimental Section
General: All reagents and solvents were purchased from commercial suppliers and used as received. Silica-alumina and Y-zeolite were obtained from Micromeritics and activated prior to use. UiO-66 and MOF-808 were synthesized via literature protocols.[96] PXRD measurements were performed on dry MOF powders using a Bruker D2 Phaser with $\mathsf{C u K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5\dot{4}]78\:\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ). XRD patterns were refined using JadePro version 9.0 and employed a baseline Eu-TCPB structure as a starting unit cell. A structureless refinement was performed where peak intensities were allowed to refine without constraint with regard to the structure. This enabled lattice parameter refinement via whole pattern fitting. IR spectra were collected using a Thermo Scientific Nicolet iS20 FTIR spectrometer with a Smart iTX diamond ATR. Samples for gas sorption were exchanged with methanol and activated at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under high vacuum for $12\mathsf{h}$ . $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms were collected on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 surface area and porosity analyzer at $77~\mathsf{K}$ using ultra-high purity nitrogen $(99.999\%$ , Matheson Tri-Gas). Isotherms were analyzed and surface areas were calculated using Micromeritics Microactive software. TGA was performed using a TA SDTQ600 instrument. Activated MOF samples were heated to $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ under continuous $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ flow. TGA-MS on the 5M-TCPB sample was collected on a Netzsch Skimmer STA 409 CD under continuous He flow. $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra were collected on a $500~\mathsf{M H z}$ Bruker Advance II Spectrometer and analyzed using Bruker Topspin software. Elemental analyses were conducted on activated MOF samples by Galbraith Laboratories, Inc.
SEM-EDX: Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) samples were deposited on carbon tape and sputter coated with Pt or Au/Pd prior to imaging. SEM images were collected using an FEI Verios 5 UC instrument at accelerating voltages between $2{\-}-5\mathsf{k}\mathsf{V}.$ Energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) data were collected at $30~\mathsf{k V}$ using an Ametek EDAX Octane Elite Super $70~\mathsf{m m}$ EDX system.
STEM-EDX: TEM images were acquired on a Thermo Fisher Scientific Titan ETEM operated at $300\mathrm{kV}$ and equipped with a spherical aberration corrector on the image forming optics and a Gatan K3 direct-electron camera. X-ray spectral images acquired from the samples were analyzed with Sandia’s Automated eXpert Spectral Image Analysis (AXSIA) multivariate statistical analysis software.[77] AXSIA first scales the data for non-uniform noise,[78] followed by multivariate statistical analysis where the factors are made to be both simple in the spectral domain (via the VARIMAX rotation) and non-negative (via multivariate curve resolution implemented with an alternating least squares approach). Each factor has a spectral shape which contains the correlated elemental information and a corresponding image which describes the location and amount of the factor.
XPS: XPS measurements were taken on a Kratos AXIS Supra operated at a base pressure better than $2\times10^{-9}$ Torr, using a monochromatic Al $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ X-ray source $(\lambda=7486.6\mathrm{eV})$ ). Surveys and high resolution scans for elements of interest were taken on each sample. Spectra were obtained using a pass energy of $160\mathrm{eV}$ for all surveys and $20\mathrm{eV}$ for the high-resolution elements, with a step size of 0.1 eV, and a large analyzer spot size. XPS data was analyzed using CasaXPS software using the Kratos F1s relative sensitivity factors for an X-ray source at $90^{\circ}$ and corrected to $\mathsf{s p3C}$ at $285\mathrm{\eV.}$
HAXPES: HAXPES data was collected on the 7-ID2 beamline at the NSLS-II at Brookhaven National Lab.
Catalytic $C O_{2}$ Epoxidation: Activated MOF powder $(0.050\ m m\circ l$ total REE ions based on formula in ref. [56]) and tetrabutylammonium bromide ( $32.2\mathrm{mg},0.700\mathrm{mmol})$ were added to a $70m L$ round-bottom Schlenk flask and taken into a $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -filled glovebox, then $(+/-){-4}$ -chlorostyrene oxide $(\mathsf{l}2\mathsf{l}\upmu\mathsf{L}$ , 1.00 mmol based density of $1.283\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ) was added as a neat liquid. The flask was sealed with a rubber septum and removed from the glovebox, then the headspace of the flask was flushed with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gas $(99.999\%$ Matheson Trigas) for $20\thinspace s$ . The flask was sealed and placed under a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -filled balloon, then heated to $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ with gentle stirring. The reaction was then opened to the atmosphere and solid hexamethylbenzene ( $\cdot18.0\mathsf{m g},0.171\mathsf{m m o l})$ was added. The contents of the flask were dissolved in $\mathsf{l}.0\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{C D C l}_{3}$ and transferred to a glass vial, then the flask was rinsed twice with $\mathsf{C D C l}_{3}$ $10.5~\mathsf{m L}$ per rinse) and the $\mathsf{C D C l}_{3}$ solutions were combined. The resulting solution was passed through a $0.2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ polytetrafluoroethylene syringe filter then analyzed by ${}^{1}{\mathsf{H N M R}}$ . The mmol of cyclic carbonate product and remaining starting material were quantified by integration of the NMR spectra with reference to the hexamethylbenzene internal standard.
